{"graph_chars": "Price ceilings are rare in labor markets. Rules that stop people earning are not popular. One exception: proposed caps on top executives. Price floors are common. The federal minimum wage is a floor: illegal to pay less. Mid two thousand nine it went to seven dollars twenty-five an hour. Forty hours a week, fifty weeks, is fourteen thousand five hundred a year, below the two thousand twenty-one poverty line for a family of four, twenty-six thousand five hundred. Cities pushed a living wage, usually a few dollars above the federal floor, often only for city workers or city contractors. Baltimore, nineteen ninety-four, was first. Figure four point four. No federal floor in the example. Equilibrium ten dollars an hour and twelve hundred city workers. A living wage of twelve dollars: sixteen hundred look for jobs, the city hires seven hundred. Surplus of labor. Those who keep a job at twelve are better off. Those who would have worked at ten and now cannot, are not.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0636], [0.0636, 0.1271], [0.1271, 0.1907], [0.1907, 0.2543], [0.2543, 0.3178], [0.3178, 0.3814], [0.3814, 0.445], [0.445, 0.5085], [0.5085, 0.5721], [0.5721, 0.6357], [0.6357, 0.6992], [0.6992, 0.7628], [0.7628, 0.8264], [0.8264, 0.8899], [0.8899, 0.9535], [0.9535, 1.017], [1.017, 1.0806], [1.0806, 1.1442], [1.1442, 1.2077], [1.2077, 1.2713], [1.2713, 1.3349], [1.3349, 1.3984], [1.3984, 1.462], [1.462, 1.5256], [1.5256, 1.5891], [1.5891, 1.6527], [1.6527, 1.7163], [1.7163, 1.7798], [1.7798, 1.8434], [1.8434, 1.907], [1.907, 1.9705], [1.9705, 2.0341], [2.0341, 2.0977], [2.0977, 2.1612], [2.1612, 2.2248], [2.2248, 2.2884], [2.2884, 2.3519], [2.3519, 2.4155], [2.4155, 2.4791], [2.4791, 2.5426], [2.5426, 2.6062], [2.6062, 2.6698], [2.6698, 2.7333], [2.7333, 2.7969], [2.7969, 2.8605], [2.8605, 2.924], [2.924, 2.9876], [2.9876, 3.0511], [3.0511, 3.1147], [3.1147, 3.1783], [3.1783, 3.2418], [3.2418, 3.3054], [3.3054, 3.369], [3.369, 3.4325], [3.4325, 3.4961], [3.4961, 3.55